@meta
id: 18-16
title: Scenario — the spec that was never written
chapter: 18
type: scenario
skills: edit-file, arglist, sub-basic, buffer-switch
par: 266
minutes: 14
vscode: New File, open template, copy, paste, find-and-replace, three more tabs
open: src/domain/customer/customer.provider.ts

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    CUS-212  CustomerProvider has no spec. Create one from the team
             template. Also: customers now have an optional phone
             number. Add it to the entity and map it in the repository.

One file is open. Everything else you locate yourself.

  1. The spec — it lives beside the provider, named after it:
     `customer.provider.spec.ts`. Start from
     `test/templates/provider.spec.ts`, read in whole. Then replace
     every `__Provider__` with `CustomerProvider` and `__file__` with
     `customer.provider`. The template file itself must not change.

  2. customer.entity.ts — `phone?: string;` directly under `name`.

  3. customer.repository.ts — in `toEntity`, directly under the `name`
     line:

         phone: record.phone ?? undefined,

The customer provider needs nothing. The order entity is there so you
can prove you edited the right one.

@keys
| `:e %:r.spec.ts` | a new file named after the current one |
| `:0r path` | read a file in above line 1 |
| `:%s/old/new/g` | replace throughout the file |
| `:args glob` | set the argument list, open the first |
| `:wn` `:n` | write and next; next |

@hints
Make the spec while you are in the provider: `:e %:r.spec.ts` names it
for you. `:0r` the template in, two substitutes, `:w`.
--
`:args src/domain/customer/*.ts` lines up the rest. The entity is
first; `:wn` writes it and moves on. `:n` past the spec and the
provider; the repository is last.
--
Both additions are `/name` Enter then `o` and the line. Autoindent
supplies the spaces; do not type them.
--
Par assumes the template is read in with one command and each of the
two new lines is typed once.

@solution
A reasonable route.

**The spec** — from the provider, `:e %:r.spec.ts` Enter opens an empty
`customer.provider.spec.ts` next door. `:0r test/templates/provider.spec.ts`
Enter, or Tab through it. `:%s/__Provider__/CustomerProvider/g`,
`:%s/__file__/customer.provider/`, `:w`.

**The rest** — `:args src/domain/customer/*.ts` Enter. You are in the
entity: `/name` Enter, `o`, `phone?: string;`, `Esc`, `:wn`. `:n` twice
(or `:last`) to the repository: `/name` Enter, `o`,
`phone: record.phone ?? undefined,`, `Esc`, `:wq`.

Nothing in the ticket said where anything was, and you never typed a
directory. The spec's path came from `%:r`. The template came in with
`:0r`. The remaining files came from one glob into the arglist, which
then walked you through them in order. That is the chapter: the editor
already knows where you are, and most of the files you want are defined
relative to that.

@start src/domain/customer/customer.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { CustomerRepository } from './customer.repository';

@injectable()
export class CustomerProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(CustomerRepository) protected customerRepository: CustomerRepository,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const customer = await this.customerRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!customer) {
      throw exception('CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return customer;
  }
}

